From: Michal Hocko <mhocko@suse.com>
Subject: mm, vmscan: flush tlb before large folio is freed
References: bsc#1250656
Patch-mainline: never (fixed differently)

Upstream has fixed this (likely) without realizing through bc2ff4cbc329 ("mm:
free folios in a batch in shrink_folio_list()".
This is a minimalistic downstream fix.

The underlying problem is that userspace application crashes on unexpected
library code. A custom crash handler sees discrepancies between what the
handler sees and what gets really executed. For a long time this looked
like a CPU bug or something that simply shouldn't ever happen.

With the latest crash https://bugzilla.suse.com/show_bug.cgi?id=1250656#c121
we have given a more insight to suspect the memory reclaim code.

shrink_folio_list gathers pages to be freed in a batch. We do flush TLB right
before the freeing is done. That is, however, the case only for regular (small)
pages. Large pages are more complicated. If they are PMD maped they go through
the splitting process and that flushes the TLB (either explicitly for anon
pages or through try_to_unmap via TTU_SPLIT_HUGE_PMD for any mapped pages). If
the large page is not PMD mapped then we do not split it and try_to_unmap will
not flush TLB because of TTU_BATCH_FLUSH so there is a tiny race window between
destroy_large_folio freeing the page and try_to_unmap_flush called when the
whole list of pages is processed.

If the freed page is reused in that time window then stale TLB entry could see
a new content that could explain issues we have seen in the bug report.

For this to happen we need page cache to be backed by large pages (not
necessarily PMD mapped) and source of memory pressure. xfs already does use
large pages if they are available. Memcg limits might be a good source for the
latter.

Reviewed-by: Pedro Falcato <pfalcato@suse.de>
Reviewed-by: Vlastimil Babka <vbabka@suse.cz>
Signed-off-by: Michal Hocko <mhocko@suse.com>

---
 mm/vmscan.c |    6 ++++--
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/mm/vmscan.c
+++ b/mm/vmscan.c
@@ -2119,10 +2119,12 @@ free_it:
 		 * Is there need to periodically free_folio_list? It would
 		 * appear not as the counts should be low
 		 */
-		if (unlikely(folio_test_large(folio)))
+		if (unlikely(folio_test_large(folio))) {
+			try_to_unmap_flush();
 			destroy_large_folio(folio);
-		else
+		} else {
 			list_add(&folio->lru, &free_folios);
+		}
 		continue;
 
 activate_locked_split:
